      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) How can we ensure that redirects are fast?\n\n\nWithout optimization, system needs to check every single pair of short and original\nURLs in the database on read. Process, is called \"full table scan,\" & can be incredibly\nslow, as the number of URLs grows into the millions or billions\n\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Add an Index\n\n→ Designate short code as the primary key. This automatically creates an index and\nadds a uniqueness constraint.\n→ We get the benefits of both indexing and data integrity, as the database will enforce\nuniqueness and optimize queries on this field.\n\n\nChallenges\n→ While disk I/O is slower than memory access, it's not prohibitively slow.\n→ SSD can handle 100,000 IOPS, which is quite fast for many applications.\n\n→ Main challenge is the sheer volume of read operations.\n→ With 100M DAU, assuming each user performs an average of 5 redirects per day,\nwe're looking at:\n\n100,000,000 users * 5 redirects = 500,000,000 redirects per day\n500,000,000 / 86,400 seconds ≈ 5,787 redirects per second\n\n→ Most redirects will occur during peak hours, so we need to design for high-traffic\nspikes. Multiplying by 100x to handle the spikes means we need to handle ~600k read\noperations per second.\n\n→ DB won't be able to keep up. High read load leads to increased response times,\npotential timeouts, & might affect other database operations like URL shortening.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implementing an In-Memory Cache (e.g., Redis)\n\n→ Cache can stores frequently accessed mappings of short codes to long URLs\n→ We could use a cache aside strategy\n\n{ shortCode: originalUrl }\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Cache invalidation can be complex, when updates or deletions occur (though this\nissue is minimized since URLs are mostly read-heavy and rarely change)\n→ Cache needs time to \"warm up,\" initial requests will still hit database until cache is\npopulated\n→ Memory limitations require careful decisions about cache size, eviction policies (e.g.,\nLRU), and which entries to store\n→ Introducing cache adds complexity to the system architecture, need to discuss\ninvalidation (TTL as backup & CDC/output table triggered by updates/deletes) and\ndiscuss the tradeoffs (to handle the higher read load, we introduce the cache)\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Leveraging Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Edge Computing\n\n→ Short URL domain is served through a CDN with Points of Presence (PoPs)\ngeographically distributed around the world\n→ CDN nodes cache short codes to long URL mappings. Redirect requests are handled\ncloser to user's location\n→ Going further, redirect logic can be deployed to edge using platforms like Cloudflare\nWorkers or AWS Lambda@Edge. Redirection happens directly at CDN level without\nreaching origin server, meaningfully reducing the latency\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Managing cache invalidation & consistency across CDN nodes can be complex.\n→ Debugging & monitoring in a distributed edge environment can be more challenging\ncompared to centralized servers\n→ Trading cost and complexity for performance. Whether or not is worth it depends on\nfactors like company price sensitivity, user experience requirements, and traffic patterns",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) How can we ensure that redirects are fast?\n\n\nWithout optimization, system needs to check every single pair of short and original URLs in the database on read. Process, is called \"full table scan,\" & can be incredibly slow, as the number of URLs grows into the millions or billions\n\n\n\nGOOD SOLUTION: Add an Index\n\n→ Designate short code as the primary key. This automatically creates an index and adds a uniqueness constraint.\n→ We get the benefits of both indexing and data integrity, as the database will enforce uniqueness and optimize queries on this field.\n\n\nChallenges\n→ While disk I/O is slower than memory access, it's not prohibitively slow.\n→ SSD can handle 100,000 IOPS, which is quite fast for many applications.\n\n→ Main challenge is the sheer volume of read operations.\n→ With 100M DAU, assuming each user performs an average of 5 redirects per day, we're looking at:\n\n100,000,000 users * 5 redirects = 500,000,000 redirects per day\n500,000,000 / 86,400 seconds ≈ 5,787 redirects per second\n\n→ Most redirects will occur during peak hours, so we need to design for high-traffic spikes. Multiplying by 100x to handle the spikes means we need to handle ~600k read operations per second.\n\n→ DB won't be able to keep up. High read load leads to increased response times, potential timeouts, & might affect other database operations like URL shortening.\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Implementing an In-Memory Cache (e.g., Redis)\n\n→ Cache can stores frequently accessed mappings of short codes to long URLs\n→ We could use a cache aside strategy\n\n{ shortCode: originalUrl }\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Cache invalidation can be complex, when updates or deletions occur (though this issue is minimized since URLs are mostly read-heavy and rarely change)\n→ Cache needs time to \"warm up,\" initial requests will still hit database until cache is populated\n→ Memory limitations require careful decisions about cache size, eviction policies (e.g., LRU), and which entries to store\n→ Introducing cache adds complexity to the system architecture, need to discuss invalidation (TTL as backup & CDC/output table triggered by updates/deletes) and discuss the tradeoffs (to handle the higher read load, we introduce the cache)\n\n\n\nGREAT SOLUTION: Leveraging Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and Edge Computing\n\n→ Short URL domain is served through a CDN with Points of Presence (PoPs) geographically distributed around the world\n→ CDN nodes cache short codes to long URL mappings. Redirect requests are handled closer to user's location\n→ Going further, redirect logic can be deployed to edge using platforms like Cloudflare Workers or AWS Lambda@Edge. Redirection happens directly at CDN level without reaching origin server, meaningfully reducing the latency\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Managing cache invalidation & consistency across CDN nodes can be complex.\n→ Debugging & monitoring in a distributed edge environment can be more challenging compared to centralized servers\n→ Trading cost and complexity for performance. Whether or not is worth it depends on factors like company price sensitivity, user experience requirements, and traffic patterns",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3YCUGV7y8MJ4GcRq52F-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4702.085228280248,
      "y": 3907.0794759831206,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 2025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1000709334,
      "version": 3757,
      "versionNonce": 1505424011,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775272773358,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) How can we scale to support 1B shortened urls and 100M DAU?\n\n\n\nSize of our database:\n→ Each row in our database consists of a \n        → short code (~8 bytes),\n        → long URL (~100 bytes),\n        → creationTime (~8 bytes),\n        → optional custom alias (~100 bytes), &\n        → expiration date (~8 bytes).\nThis totals to ~200 bytes per row. We can round up to 500 bytes to account for any\nadditional metadata like the creator id, analytics id, etc.\n→ 1B mappings, we're looking at 500 bytes * 1B rows = 500GB of data\n→ If we were to hit a hardware limit, we could always shard our data across multiple\nservers but a single Postgres instance, for example, should do for now\n\n\n\nSo what database technology should we use?\n\n→ We could estimate that maybe 100k new urls are created per day. 100k new rows\nper day is ~1 row per second (100,000 ÷ 24 hours = ~4,167 per hour → 4,167 ÷ 60\nminutes = ~69 per minute → 69 ÷ 60 seconds = ~1.15 per second)\n→  any reasonable database technology should do (ie. Postgres, MySQL, DynamoDB,\netc).\n\n\n\nBut what if the DB goes down?\n\n→ Few different strategies to ensure high availability\n        → Database Replication: create multiple identical copies of our database on\ndifferent servers. If one server goes down, we can redirect to another\n        → Database Backup: backup system that periodically takes a snapshot of our\ndatabase and stores it in a separate location\n→ Both add operational complexity & overhead\n\n\n\nSeparate the services to read & write, allows us to horizontally scale each service\nindependently based on their specific demands\n\n\n\nBut what about our counter?\n→ Overhead of additional network request is negligible compared to time taken to\nperform other operations\n→ Counter needs to be accessible to all instances of the Write Service so that they can\nall agree on the next value\n→ Redis instance can be used to store counter and any other metadata that needs to be\nshared across instances of the Write Service\n→ Redis is single-threaded and is very fast for this use case, also supports atomic\nincrement operations\n\n\n\nBut should we be concerned about the overhead of an additional network request for\neach new write request?\n\n→ Could use a technique called \"counter batching\" to reduce the number of network\nrequests\n        → Write Service instance requests a batch of counter values from Redis (e.g., 1000\nvalues at a time).\n        → Redis instance increments the counter by 1000 & returns start of the batch.\n        → Write Service instance can use these 1000 values locally without needing to\ncontact Redis for each new URL\n        → When the batch is exhausted, the Write Service requests a new batch.\n\n→ To ensure HA of counter service, can use Redis Sentinel or Redis Cluster with\nautomatic failover. A single Redis instance can handle 100k+ operations per second, far\nexceeding typical URL shortening rates, especially with counter batching.\n\n→ For multi-region deployment, allocate disjoint counter ranges to each region (e.g.,\nregion A gets 0-1B, region B gets 1B-2B) to avoid cross-region coordination. Writes go\nto the local region's Redis, while reads can be served globally via distributed caches.\n\n→ If Redis fails before replicating the latest counter, you might lose a few values, but\nsince we only need uniqueness (not continuity), this is acceptable. The database's\nUNIQUE constraint on short_code provides the ultimate safety net.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) How can we scale to support 1B shortened urls and 100M DAU?\n\n\n\nSize of our database:\n→ Each row in our database consists of a \n        → short code (~8 bytes),\n        → long URL (~100 bytes),\n        → creationTime (~8 bytes),\n        → optional custom alias (~100 bytes), &\n        → expiration date (~8 bytes).\nThis totals to ~200 bytes per row. We can round up to 500 bytes to account for any additional metadata like the creator id, analytics id, etc.\n→ 1B mappings, we're looking at 500 bytes * 1B rows = 500GB of data\n→ If we were to hit a hardware limit, we could always shard our data across multiple servers but a single Postgres instance, for example, should do for now\n\n\n\nSo what database technology should we use?\n\n→ We could estimate that maybe 100k new urls are created per day. 100k new rows per day is ~1 row per second (100,000 ÷ 24 hours = ~4,167 per hour → 4,167 ÷ 60 minutes = ~69 per minute → 69 ÷ 60 seconds = ~1.15 per second)\n→  any reasonable database technology should do (ie. Postgres, MySQL, DynamoDB, etc).\n\n\n\nBut what if the DB goes down?\n\n→ Few different strategies to ensure high availability\n        → Database Replication: create multiple identical copies of our database on different servers. If one server goes down, we can redirect to another\n        → Database Backup: backup system that periodically takes a snapshot of our database and stores it in a separate location\n→ Both add operational complexity & overhead\n\n\n\nSeparate the services to read & write, allows us to horizontally scale each service independently based on their specific demands\n\n\n\nBut what about our counter?\n→ Overhead of additional network request is negligible compared to time taken to perform other operations\n→ Counter needs to be accessible to all instances of the Write Service so that they can all agree on the next value\n→ Redis instance can be used to store counter and any other metadata that needs to be shared across instances of the Write Service\n→ Redis is single-threaded and is very fast for this use case, also supports atomic increment operations\n\n\n\nBut should we be concerned about the overhead of an additional network request for each new write request?\n\n→ Could use a technique called \"counter batching\" to reduce the number of network requests\n        → Write Service instance requests a batch of counter values from Redis (e.g., 1000 values at a time).\n        → Redis instance increments the counter by 1000 & returns start of the batch.\n        → Write Service instance can use these 1000 values locally without needing to contact Redis for each new URL\n        → When the batch is exhausted, the Write Service requests a new batch.\n\n→ To ensure HA of counter service, can use Redis Sentinel or Redis Cluster with automatic failover. A single Redis instance can handle 100k+ operations per second, far exceeding typical URL shortening rates, especially with counter batching.\n\n→ For multi-region deployment, allocate disjoint counter ranges to each region (e.g., region A gets 0-1B, region B gets 1B-2B) to avoid cross-region coordination. Writes go to the local region's Redis, while reads can be served globally via distributed caches.\n\n→ If Redis fails before replicating the latest counter, you might lose a few values, but since we only need uniqueness (not continuity), this is acceptable. The database's UNIQUE constraint on short_code provides the ultimate safety net.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
