      "versionNonce": 67886189,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775849923100,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "→ ⚡ Redis is particularly well-suited for managing this counter because it's single-\nthreaded and supports atomic operations.\n→ 🧵 Being single-threaded means Redis processes one command at a time, eliminating\nrace conditions.\n→ 🔢 Its INCR command atomically increments the counter and returns the new value in a\nsingle operation.\n→ 🔒 Because Redis is single-threaded, two simultaneous calls will always receive\ndifferent values.\n→ 📊 If one gets 1000, the other gets 1001.\n→ ✅ This guarantee is what makes Redis ideal for distributed counter management.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "DmIRymBrkxfkqype2EHwu",
      "originalText": "→ ⚡ Redis is particularly well-suited for managing this counter because it's single-threaded and supports atomic operations.\n→ 🧵 Being single-threaded means Redis processes one command at a time, eliminating race conditions.\n→ 🔢 Its INCR command atomically increments the counter and returns the new value in a single operation.\n→ 🔒 Because Redis is single-threaded, two simultaneous calls will always receive different values.\n→ 📊 If one gets 1000, the other gets 1001.\n→ ✅ This guarantee is what makes Redis ideal for distributed counter management.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Lm_03pr_eFEx2GGfTsUu3",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 1753.5787317565414,
      "y": 6985.630789412357,
      "width": 825.5660271246614,
      "height": 343.9723546851792,
