      "locked": false,
      "text": "→ 🎯 Prioritize consistency over availability only if every read must receive the most\nrecent write; otherwise, the system will break.\n\n→ 📈 Example: Stock Trading App — if a user buys a share of AAPL in Germany 🇩🇪 and\nthen another user immediately tries to buy a share of AAPL in the US 🇺🇸, you need to be\nsure that the first transaction has been replicated to the US before you can proceed. ✅\n\n→ 📁 Example: Dropbox — it's okay if a user in Germany 🇩🇪 uploads a file and a user in\nthe US 🇺🇸 can't see it for a few seconds. ⏳😴",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "drsrvDXe_9StgowHKlSNW",
      "originalText": "→ 🎯 Prioritize consistency over availability only if every read must receive the most recent write; otherwise, the system will break.\n\n→ 📈 Example: Stock Trading App — if a user buys a share of AAPL in Germany 🇩🇪 and then another user immediately tries to buy a share of AAPL in the US 🇺🇸, you need to be sure that the first transaction has been replicated to the US before you can proceed. ✅\n\n→ 📁 Example: Dropbox — it's okay if a user in Germany 🇩🇪 uploads a file and a user in the US 🇺🇸 can't see it for a few seconds. ⏳😴",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "fHGzIDoihJWo3f2jE8Tza",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1851.965122457193,
      "y": -1244.7420898696414,
      "width": 959.5675262266183,
      "height": 1900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2W",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1242883274,
      "version": 222,
      "versionNonce": 887623510,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1775312459359,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Users should be able to upload a file from any device\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Upload File to a Single Server\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📤 POST /files accepts the file and metadata\n→ 💾 File stored on the server's local file system\n→ 🗄️ Metadata saved in the database\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Simple to implement for small applications\n→ 📈 As files grow, need to keep adding storage or horizontally scale with more servers\n→ 💀 Not reliable — if server goes down, we lose access to all files\n→ 🔒 Single point of failure — no redundancy\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Store File in Blob Storage (e.g. S3, Google Cloud Storage)\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📤 User uploads file to backend, backend forwards it to Blob Storage\n→ 🗄️ Metadata saved in database\n→ ♾️ Virtually unlimited storage — scaling handled automatically\n→ 🛡️ If server goes down, files are still safe in Blob Storage\n→ 🔄 Can leverage lifecycle policies (auto-delete old files) and versioning\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Much more reliable and scalable than single server\n→ 🔁 File is uploaded twice — once to backend, once to Blob Storage (redundant)\n→ 🧩 More complex — need to handle edge cases:\n        → 📤 File uploaded but metadata not saved\n        → 🗄️ Metadata saved but file not uploaded\n→ 🔐 Solved with a transactional approach — only save metadata if file successfully uploaded and vice\nversa\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Upload File Directly to Blob Storage via Presigned URLs\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔑 Backend generates a presigned URL — a temporary URL giving the user permission to upload\ndirectly to a specific location in Blob Storage\n→ ⚡ File goes straight from client to Blob Storage — backend never touches the file\n→ 📊 Metadata saved with status \"uploading\" immediately\n→ 🔔 Blob Storage sends a notification (S3 Notifications) to backend once upload is complete\n→ ✅ Backend updates metadata status to \"uploaded\"\n\n📋 3-Step Process\n\n→ 1️⃣ Client requests presigned URL from backend\n\nPOST /files/presigned-url\nRequest: { FileMetadata }\nResponse: PresignedUrl\n\n→ 2️⃣ Client uploads file directly to Blob Storage via PUT request to the presigned URL\n→ 3️⃣ Blob Storage notifies backend → metadata status updated to \"uploaded\" ✅\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Fastest and cheapest — no redundant uploads through backend\n→ ✅ Reduces load on backend server\n→ ✅ Scales effortlessly — Blob Storage handles everything\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ 🔔 Relies on Blob Storage notifications being reliable\n→ 🔑 Presigned URLs must be managed carefully — they are temporary and grant upload access to\nanyone who has them",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Users should be able to upload a file from any device\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Upload File to a Single Server\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📤 POST /files accepts the file and metadata\n→ 💾 File stored on the server's local file system\n→ 🗄️ Metadata saved in the database\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Simple to implement for small applications\n→ 📈 As files grow, need to keep adding storage or horizontally scale with more servers\n→ 💀 Not reliable — if server goes down, we lose access to all files\n→ 🔒 Single point of failure — no redundancy\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Store File in Blob Storage (e.g. S3, Google Cloud Storage)\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 📤 User uploads file to backend, backend forwards it to Blob Storage\n→ 🗄️ Metadata saved in database\n→ ♾️ Virtually unlimited storage — scaling handled automatically\n→ 🛡️ If server goes down, files are still safe in Blob Storage\n→ 🔄 Can leverage lifecycle policies (auto-delete old files) and versioning\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Much more reliable and scalable than single server\n→ 🔁 File is uploaded twice — once to backend, once to Blob Storage (redundant)\n→ 🧩 More complex — need to handle edge cases:\n        → 📤 File uploaded but metadata not saved\n        → 🗄️ Metadata saved but file not uploaded\n→ 🔐 Solved with a transactional approach — only save metadata if file successfully uploaded and vice versa\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Upload File Directly to Blob Storage via Presigned URLs\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔑 Backend generates a presigned URL — a temporary URL giving the user permission to upload directly to a specific location in Blob Storage\n→ ⚡ File goes straight from client to Blob Storage — backend never touches the file\n→ 📊 Metadata saved with status \"uploading\" immediately\n→ 🔔 Blob Storage sends a notification (S3 Notifications) to backend once upload is complete\n→ ✅ Backend updates metadata status to \"uploaded\"\n\n📋 3-Step Process\n\n→ 1️⃣ Client requests presigned URL from backend\n\nPOST /files/presigned-url\nRequest: { FileMetadata }\nResponse: PresignedUrl\n\n→ 2️⃣ Client uploads file directly to Blob Storage via PUT request to the presigned URL\n→ 3️⃣ Blob Storage notifies backend → metadata status updated to \"uploaded\" ✅\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Fastest and cheapest — no redundant uploads through backend\n→ ✅ Reduces load on backend server\n→ ✅ Scales effortlessly — Blob Storage handles everything\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ 🔔 Relies on Blob Storage notifications being reliable\n→ 🔑 Presigned URLs must be managed carefully — they are temporary and grant upload access to anyone who has them",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
