      "locked": false,
      "text": "4) Users can automatically sync files across devices\n\n\n🔄 File Sync Across Devices\n\nTwo directions to sync:\n→ 📤 Local → Remote (user edits file on device, push to cloud)\n→ 📥 Remote → Local (cloud changes, pull to device)\n\n\n\n📤 Local → Remote\n\n🔧 Approach: Client-Side Sync Agent\n\n→ 👀 Monitors local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events:\n        → 🪟 FileSystemWatcher on Windows\n        → 🍎 FSEvents on macOS\n→ 📋 When change detected, queues the modified file for upload locally\n→ 📡 Uses upload API to send changes to server along with updated metadata\n→ ⚔️ Conflicts resolved using \"last write wins\" — most recent edit is saved\n\n\n📝 Note on Versioning\n→ 🔢 Out of scope here, but typically you wouldn't overwrite the existing file\n→ ➕ Instead, add a new file (or new chunks) and update a version number and pointer on the metadata\n\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Remote server is the single source of truth — changes propagated as soon as possible\n→ ⚔️ Last write wins is simple but can cause data loss if two users edit simultaneously\n→ 🧩 OS-specific file system events add complexity across platforms\n\n\n\n📥 Remote → Local\n\nEach client needs to know when remote changes happen so it can pull them down. Three approaches:\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Polling\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ⏰ Client periodically asks the server \"has anything changed since my last sync?\"\n→ 🗄️ Server queries DB for files with updatedAt timestamp newer than last sync time\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Simple to implement\n→ 🐢 Slow to detect changes — only as fast as the polling interval\n→ 📡 Wastes bandwidth — sends requests even when nothing has changed\n→ 📈 Doesn't scale well with many connected clients\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: WebSocket or SSE\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Server maintains an open connection with each client\n→ 📤 Pushes notifications to client when changes occur in real-time\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Real-time updates — near instant sync\n→ ✅ No wasted bandwidth — server only sends data when something changes\n→ 🧩 More complex to implement than polling\n→ 💀 WebSocket connections can drop — missed messages mean missed updates\n→ 🔒 No guaranteed delivery — not reliable enough on its own\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Hybrid (WebSocket + Polling)\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Each client maintains a single WebSocket (or SSE) connection per device/session — not one per file\n→ 📤 Server pushes change notifications for all files the user has access to through this connection\n→ 🛡️ Polling acts as a safety net to catch any missed changes\n\n→ ⚡ Active Notification — server pushes change events through WebSocket in real-time as they happen\n→ 🔁 Periodic Polling — client also polls every few minutes using:\n\nGET /files/changes?since={timestamp}\n→ catches any changes missed due to dropped WebSocket connections\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Best of both worlds — real-time updates with a reliable fallback\n→ ✅ Guarantees eventual consistency even if WebSocket was temporarily interrupted\n→ ✅ Single connection per device — efficient and scalable\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ ⚙️ Need to manage both WebSocket connections and polling logic on the client\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4) Users can automatically sync files across devices\n\n\n🔄 File Sync Across Devices\n\nTwo directions to sync:\n→ 📤 Local → Remote (user edits file on device, push to cloud)\n→ 📥 Remote → Local (cloud changes, pull to device)\n\n\n\n📤 Local → Remote\n\n🔧 Approach: Client-Side Sync Agent\n\n→ 👀 Monitors local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events:\n        → 🪟 FileSystemWatcher on Windows\n        → 🍎 FSEvents on macOS\n→ 📋 When change detected, queues the modified file for upload locally\n→ 📡 Uses upload API to send changes to server along with updated metadata\n→ ⚔️ Conflicts resolved using \"last write wins\" — most recent edit is saved\n\n\n📝 Note on Versioning\n→ 🔢 Out of scope here, but typically you wouldn't overwrite the existing file\n→ ➕ Instead, add a new file (or new chunks) and update a version number and pointer on the metadata\n\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Remote server is the single source of truth — changes propagated as soon as possible\n→ ⚔️ Last write wins is simple but can cause data loss if two users edit simultaneously\n→ 🧩 OS-specific file system events add complexity across platforms\n\n\n\n📥 Remote → Local\n\nEach client needs to know when remote changes happen so it can pull them down. Three approaches:\n\n\n\n❌ Bad Solution: Polling\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ ⏰ Client periodically asks the server \"has anything changed since my last sync?\"\n→ 🗄️ Server queries DB for files with updatedAt timestamp newer than last sync time\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Simple to implement\n→ 🐢 Slow to detect changes — only as fast as the polling interval\n→ 📡 Wastes bandwidth — sends requests even when nothing has changed\n→ 📈 Doesn't scale well with many connected clients\n\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: WebSocket or SSE\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Server maintains an open connection with each client\n→ 📤 Pushes notifications to client when changes occur in real-time\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Real-time updates — near instant sync\n→ ✅ No wasted bandwidth — server only sends data when something changes\n→ 🧩 More complex to implement than polling\n→ 💀 WebSocket connections can drop — missed messages mean missed updates\n→ 🔒 No guaranteed delivery — not reliable enough on its own\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Hybrid (WebSocket + Polling)\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ 🔌 Each client maintains a single WebSocket (or SSE) connection per device/session — not one per file\n→ 📤 Server pushes change notifications for all files the user has access to through this connection\n→ 🛡️ Polling acts as a safety net to catch any missed changes\n\n→ ⚡ Active Notification — server pushes change events through WebSocket in real-time as they happen\n→ 🔁 Periodic Polling — client also polls every few minutes using:\n\nGET /files/changes?since={timestamp}\n→ catches any changes missed due to dropped WebSocket connections\n\n⚠️ Tradeoffs\n\n→ ✅ Best of both worlds — real-time updates with a reliable fallback\n→ ✅ Guarantees eventual consistency even if WebSocket was temporarily interrupted\n→ ✅ Single connection per device — efficient and scalable\n→ 🧩 Most complex of the three approaches\n→ ⚙️ Need to manage both WebSocket connections and polling logic on the client\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
